    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_0Sy4z7UTkBM0ptI",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID51",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Dr. Simone Tran In a cozy office, Dr. Simone Tran, a science communication expert, sits surrounde...",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<div class=\"trust-content-container\">\n<div class=\"avatar\"><img src=\"https:\/\/uwaterloo.ca1.qualtrics.com\/CP\/Graphic.php?IM=IM_7WzmDcqMHUbCIzc\" height=\"120\" alt=\"Author Avatar\"><\/div>\n\n<div class=\"author-description\">\n<h2>Dr. Simone Tran<\/h2>\n\n<p>In a cozy office, Dr. Simone Tran, a science communication expert, sits surrounded by stacks of research papers and a glowing laptop screen. With keen insight and passion, she delves into the latest studies, distilling complex data into engaging narratives. Her fingers dance across the keyboard, crafting articles that transform technical jargon into accessible, compelling stories. She pauses to consider her audience, ensuring clarity and impact in every word. Colleagues often seek her input, admiring her ability to make science resonate with the public. Dr. Tran\u2019s dedication shines through her work, bridging the gap between researchers and everyday readers.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"blog-post-content\">\n<h1>Revolutionary New Medication Shows Promising Results in Recent Study<\/h1>\n\n<p>Exciting news is emerging from the Horizon Medical Institute, where researchers have unveiled promising results for a new medication named <b>Velocerin<\/b>. This innovative drug targets inflammatory conditions such as rheumatoid arthritis and inflammatory bowel disease, showing significant potential to improve patients' lives.<\/p>\n\n<p>Velocerin works by specifically inhibiting the TNF-alpha protein, a key player in the inflammation process. Unlike traditional treatments that often cause broad, undesirable side effects, Velocerin's targeted approach allows it to reduce inflammation more effectively and with greater safety.<\/p>\n\n<p>The study involved over 1,000 participants who received either Velocerin or a placebo over six months. The results were compelling: those on Velocerin experienced a <b>60% reduction in symptoms<\/b>, compared to a <b>25% reduction<\/b> in the placebo group. Additionally, participants reported substantial improvements in their quality of life, citing reduced pain and enhanced mobility. Importantly, the drug was well-tolerated, with only mild side effects such as headaches and fatigue reported by a small number of users.<\/p>\n\n<p>For individuals living with chronic inflammatory conditions, Velocerin offers hope for a future with better symptom management and fewer side effects. While these findings are encouraging, further trials are necessary to confirm the drug's efficacy and safety on a larger scale. As research progresses, Velocerin stands out as a potential breakthrough in the treatment of inflammatory diseases, promising a brighter, healthier future for many.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "High",
        "QuestionType": "DB",
        "Selector": "TB",
        "DataVisibility": {
          "Private": false,
          "Hidden": false
        },
        "Configuration": {
          "QuestionDescriptionOption": "UseText"
        },
        "QuestionDescription": "Dr. Simone Tran In a cozy office, Dr. Simone Tran, a science communication expert, sits surrounde...",
        "ChoiceOrder": [],
        "Validation": {
          "Settings": {
            "Type": "None"
          }
        },
        "GradingData": [],
        "Language": [],
        "NextChoiceId": 4,
        "NextAnswerId": 1,
        "QuestionID": "QID51",
